88	   Acts cut the page into pinned blocks, which is the right shape for a page of
89	   chapters and the wrong shape for one continuous camera move: the reader hits
90	   the end of an act, the stage unsticks, a static page slides past, and the next
91	   act starts the whole thing again. Worldflight removes the seams by removing
92	   the blocks. There is ONE fixed stage for the entire page. The only element in
93	   document flow is a spacer, whose height the engine sets to the sum of the
94	   segment weights plus one viewport so the last flight can finish. Scroll drives
95	   the film timeline and the overlay opacity. Nothing else moves.
96	
97	     data-sc-w        viewport-heights this segment owns. Default 1.3.
98	     data-sc-linger   dwell remap for this leg only (see lingerEase). Max 0.6.
99	     data-sc-seam     crossfade band, in viewport-heights of scroll. Default 0.12.
100	     data-sc-waypoint label published on --sc-seg / the sc:waypoint event, so a
101	                      page can draw its own route rail. The engine draws none.
102	     data-sc-window   on a copy block: "hero" | "finale" | "from to [in [out]]"
103	                      as fractions of the WHOLE track.
104	
105	   Every clip stays mounted for the life of the page. Segments crossfade by
106	   opacity over the seam band; nothing ever swaps a src, because a src swap is a
107	   black frame and a black frame is the cut this mode exists to avoid.
108	
109	   ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
110	   POINTER: interactivity that isn't scroll
111	   ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
112	     data-sc-tilt="8"         3D tilt toward the pointer, spring-damped, degrees.
113	     data-sc-magnet="0.35"    element drifts toward the pointer inside its bounds.
114	     data-sc-spotlight        publishes --sc-mx/--sc-my (0..1) for a light that
115	                              follows the pointer across the surface.
116	   All three are gated to (hover: hover) and (pointer: fine) and disabled under
117	   prefers-reduced-motion. Touch never fires them.
118	
119	   ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
120	   REDUCED MOTION
121	   ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
122	   Fewer and gentler, not zero. Cues still fade (comprehension survives), but
123	   translation collapses, video clips are never fetched (the poster holds), and
124	   pointer devices are inert. A worldflight still tells its whole story: the
125	   posters cross-dissolve through the same seams and the same copy windows.
126	
127	   ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
128	   THE PLAYHEAD
